      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)={t}^{2}+t-12,\\qquad y'(t)=-3{t}^{2}+9t-6.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{t}^{2}+9t-6 & =0 \\\\ -3(t-2)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(1) & =-10\\ne 0, \\\\ x'(2) & =-6\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=1$ and $t=2$.",
