      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=-{t}^{2}+5t-4,\\qquad y'(t)=-6{t}^{2}-30t-24.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{t}^{2}-30t-24 & =0 \\\\ -6(t+1)(t+4) & =0 \\\\ t & =-4,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-4) & =-40\\ne 0, \\\\ x'(-1) & =-10\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-4$ and $t=-1$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/id": "ef577fcb-0999-5945-bf43-978372f3d503"
    },
