      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2{t}^{2}+2t-4,\\qquad y'(t)=6{t}^{2}+42t+72.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}+42t+72 & =0 \\\\ 6(t+3)(t+4) & =0 \\\\ t & =-4,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-4) & =20\\ne 0, \\\\ x'(-3) & =8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-4$ and $t=-3$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/id": "27e96b82-b0d7-5e43-a3b4-62081928ed26"
    },
